EXT. SALT FLATS - ABANDONED EVAPORATION WORKS - DAY

A white plain stretches to a white sky. Rotting wooden vats lie in rows like open coffins. Heat shimmers off the crust.

SILAS WHITTAKER (50s, gray beard, duster bleached by sun) kneels beside a dead horse. He runs one hand along its neck and says nothing.

Behind him, a shadow lengthens. He doesn't turn.

CONSTANCE "CONNIE" REYES (30s, wide-brimmed hat, rifle across her back) stands twenty feet away on the crust. She holds a canteen by its strap.

                    CONNIE
          That your horse?

                    SILAS
          Was.

                    CONNIE
          Looks like the salt got her.

                    SILAS
          Looks like a bullet got her.

She walks closer. Stops short of arm's reach. He rises, brushing his knees, and finally turns. His eyes go to the canteen. Hers go to his holster.

                    CONNIE
          I'll trade you water for a name.

                    SILAS
          I'd trade you a name for a mule.

                    CONNIE
          I don't have a mule.

                    SILAS
          Then you don't have a name worth the water.

She almost smiles. He almost doesn't. The wind pulls at her hat brim. She tilts the canteen. Water slaps against the metal, loud in the silence.

                    SILAS (CONT'D)
          Don't wave that around.

                    CONNIE
          It's mine to wave.

He takes a step. She does not move. He takes another. Her hand drifts back to the rifle sling.

                    SILAS
          Easy.

                    CONNIE
          You first.

They stand with the dead horse between them and the white sky above. Neither one blinks.

                    SILAS
          You work for Dutch Halloran?

                    CONNIE
          Never heard of him.

                    SILAS
          That's a lie, and you tell it badly.

                    CONNIE
          Then I'm in good company.

He nods slowly. Looks off toward a ridge on the horizon, a thin dark line.

                    SILAS
          He's coming, you know. He's got six men and a Gatling gun on a wagon.

                    CONNIE
          I know.

                    SILAS
          Then why are you standing here?

She uncaps the canteen. Drinks. Wipes her mouth with the back of her wrist. Holds it out to him at arm's length, the way one offers food to a dog.

                    CONNIE
          Same reason as you.

He takes it. Drinks long. The water runs down his beard. He hands it back.

                    SILAS
          He killed my brother.

                    CONNIE
          He killed my husband.

                    SILAS
          He burned my ranch.

                    CONNIE
          He took my land.

They speak at once, the words overlapping, neither stopping for the other.

                    SILAS
          I came out here to die.
                    ^
                    CONNIE
          I came out here to finish it.

A beat. They look at each other, really look, for the first time.

                    SILAS
          Can you shoot that thing?

                    CONNIE
          Better than you can shoot that.

He glances at his holster. Almost laughs.

                    SILAS
          What's your plan?

She points past him at the nearest vat, a long wooden trough sunk in the crust.

                    CONNIE
          Wagon comes down the old cart road. Wheels sink in the brine. Horses panic.

                    SILAS
          And the Gatling?

                    CONNIE
          Pointed at the sky, if the wagon tips.

He studies the vats. The road. The ridge. The long, long distance.

                    SILAS
          That's a lot of ifs.

                    CONNIE
          You got a better one?

He doesn't. He pulls the revolver from his holster and checks the cylinder. Three rounds. He sighs.

                    SILAS
          Hand me a few.

She digs in her coat. Pours a small handful of cartridges into his palm. He loads them one by one.

On the ridge, a plume of dust rises. A glint of brass. The faint creak of wheels.

                    CONNIE
          Time to pick a vat.

She walks away toward the trough. He lingers over the dead horse for one last moment, touches its mane, and follows.
